course: dictionary
lesson: 4291
title: A red syrup in a drink
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Grenadine is a noun. It is a sweet red syrup, traditionally made from pomegranate juice, and people use it to flavour and colour drinks. You cannot count it like apples, so we say «a splash of grenadine» or «some grenadine», not «three grenadines».
explain: Look for the clues around the word. «Un diabolo grenadine is red.» The word red tells you the colour, and the word diabolo tells you it is a drink. A diabolo is fizzy lemonade with a syrup, so grenadine must be the syrup that makes it red.
explain: In a science lesson, grenadine can show density. When you pour grenadine gently into a glass of lemonade or orange juice, the heavy syrup sinks to the bottom and the drink forms red layers. Heavy things sink, and light things float on top.
fact: Grenadine is a noun, and it is uncountable.
fact: Grenadine is a sweet red syrup, traditionally made from pomegranate juice.
fact: People add grenadine to lemonade or orange juice to make red, layered drinks.
fact: «Un diabolo grenadine» is a fizzy lemonade with red grenadine syrup.
fact: Phrases to remember: «a splash of grenadine» and «grenadine syrup».
exercise: «Un diabolo grenadine is red.» What colour is the drink made with grenadine? | Blue | Yellow | *Red | Green
exercise: What is grenadine? | *A sweet red syrup used to flavour and colour drinks | A type of bread | A fizzy lemonade | A sour green fruit
exercise: In «a splash of grenadine», what does splash suggest? | A loud noise | *A small amount added | A big bath | Ice cubes
test: Why does the grenadine sink to the bottom of a layered drink? | Because it floats | *Because it is heavy and dense | Because it is hot | Because it is green
test: Which sentence uses grenadine correctly? | I added a grenadines to my lemonade. | *I added some grenadine to my lemonade. | I added five grenadine bottles of sugar. | I added two grenadines to my lemonade.
exam: Grenadine is traditionally made from which fruit? | Mint | Strawberry | Lemon | *Pomegranate
